  "narrative": "The 1970s was a transformative time for Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture. As the natural hair movement gained momentum, many began to reject chemical relaxers and instead celebrate their afro-textured locks in all their glory. The afro, a hairstyle created by combing out natural growth or styling with chemical curling products, became a symbol of black pride and identity. Cornrows, a traditional African braiding practice, also experienced a resurgence in popularity during this era. Other hairstyles like box braids, which were installed using synthetic hair to add thickness and protect the natural locks, and dreadlocks, which could form naturally or be created through techniques like twisting and backcombing, also gained traction. These styles not only served as a means of self-expression but also provided a sense of community and solidarity among Black women. Unfortunately, documentation from this era is limited, and we can only speculate on the extent to which these hairstyles were adopted by African women outside of the diaspora. Nonetheless, it is clear that the 1970s marked an important turning point in the history of Black hair culture, one that paved the way for future generations of women to celebrate their natural beauty and reject Eurocentric standards of beauty.",
